A||||4||Bluebottlejunky||Alternator help?||||1||
Z||000000||Bluebottlejunky||03-28-2001||06:48 PM||||Hey every one I got my new alternator in a few days ago.  When we got it in and test drove it, it started to squeek? But then it would go away and come back later...Ya think the alternator blet needs to tightend? Any one else had this happen?  Thanks<P>------------------<BR>Mike<BR>85' GT 4sp||||reg||1
Z||000001||Indiana_resto_guy||03-28-2001||06:53 PM||||Yes, tighten the belt.||||reg||1
Z||000002||87MaroonGTKCMO||03-29-2001||12:51 AM||||Did you replace the belt at the same time you replaced the Alternator? It's well worth the hassle to just buy a new one. If this is a 4 cyl car 84-86 v-belt type, these are the worst. Make sure you didn't tilt the alternator during the install and that the alternator bracket is not bent.<P>Last but not least, buy a brand name belt!<BR>P.S.&gt;Overtightening can shorten the life of the belt. The more you tighten and stretch it out, the worse the problem gets.<P>I've had to change those pesky things several times in the past if they got wet or greasy.<P>Fieroless, but still lookin!<BR>Sean<P><p>[This message has been edited by 87MaroonGTKCMO (edited 03-29-2001).]||||reg||3
Z||000003||pontiacs4me||03-29-2001||01:44 AM||||I had this problem about a month back.  I had a brand new belt on it that wouldnt shut up.  It was driving me insane.  Anyway I went and bougt a new one, it did the same thing after about 3 days of driving.  So I checked the belt tension and it was still pretty tight, but not as tight as the day I put it on.  New belts dont have the grooves cut in as deep as worn out ones do.  So I tightened it again.  After about 3 days it started squealing again, so I tightened it again, and that time it stopped for good.  I have a v6 I dont know if you do too, but in case you do Ill tell you what I did to get mine real tight, (I dont care if it shortens the life of it by being too tight, theres nothing more emabarrasing in a parking lot full of chicks, when you fire up your car and it squeals all the way out of the parking lot)  anyway, take off the passenger side wheel, and take the 13mm bolt loose, take the lug bar and put it in the adjustment slot.  pry against the bottom of the strut area at the reinforcement.  Pull that thing as hard as you can, and have a buddy tighten the bolt.  Or if your really talented you can do it yourself.  If its a new belt you may have to do it a couple of times before it quits for good.  good luck||||reg||1
Z||000004||Bluebottlejunky||03-29-2001||03:10 PM||||Thanks alot everone yes my car is a V-6, no I didn't get a new blet because the old one was in great shape..thanks for the help pontiacs4me i will have to try that.<P><P>------------------<BR>Mike<BR>85' GT 4sp||||reg||1
